# Line endings, and why this file exists.
#
# Several tests in the Rust shell read their OWN SOURCE with `include_str!` and
# search it for `\n`-anchored patterns — the source pins that catch a call being
# deleted out of a function where no runtime assertion can reach (see
# `the_pump_asks_dispatch_off_loop_before_it_awaits_anything`). Git on Windows
# checks out CRLF by default, so `body.find("\n}\n")` finds nothing and four such
# tests failed on the Windows runner's first ever run (2026-08-24) for a reason
# that had nothing to do with what they guard.
#
# `eol=lf` makes every checkout agree with the bytes in the repository. The tests
# ALSO normalise CRLF themselves, deliberately: a gate that is green because of a
# checkout setting is green for a reason outside the code it guards, and this
# repository's own rule is that such a gate is worse than a red one. This file
# fixes the working copy; the normalisation fixes the test.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, never touched.
*.png binary
*.icns binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
